= What people can still be paid for <ch-residual>

The wage ceiling of @ch-wages applies wherever a machine is a substitute for a person. There is one kind of work to which it does not apply even under the full premise, and this chapter measures how much that exception is worth.

== Where the buyer wants a person

#definition([Human-required work])[
  Work is human-required when the buyer's purpose is not served by a machine however capable the machine is, either because a human being is part of what the buyer wants, or because a rule demands that a human being do it.
] <d-human-required>

It helps to sort this work by the reason a person is required.

- *Presence.* Some services are valued because another person is there: much of the care of small children and the old, the work of clergy, coaches and mentors, hospitality at its best, companionship. A machine may do every task involved and still not be what was wanted.
- *Contest and performance.* People want to watch people. Computers have played chess better than any human since 1997, and far more people now follow human chess than did then; almost nobody watches computers play each other. The same holds for sport, live music and theater.
- *Provenance.* Some goods are valued for where they came from: a handmade table, a painting by a known artist, a letter someone wrote themselves. The machine-made version may be better in every measurable respect and still be a different product.
- *Accountability.* Some roles exist so that a person answers for a decision: the judge, the juror, the elected official, the company director, the engineer who signs the drawing. We want someone who can be blamed, sued, dismissed or voted out.
- *Legal reservation.* The law reserves some work for licensed people: prescribing drugs, arguing in court, flying passengers. This lasts as long as the law does.
- *Status.* Human service can be bought as a display of wealth precisely because it is unnecessary: the butler, the tailor, the concierge. The economist Thorstein Veblen described the pattern in 1899.

These are real, and under the premise they are all that is left. Wages will not be zero. The question is whether this work can do what the labor market does today, which is to give most households most of their income.

== The arithmetic of human-required work

Under the premise, the only wages paid are for human-required work. That gives a simple identity.

#proposition([The wage share equals the human-required share of spending], from: [@p-identity, @p-wages-fall, @d-human-required])[
  In a post-labor economy, total wages equal total spending on human-required work. The share of national income paid as wages is therefore equal to the share of all spending that buyers choose, or are obliged by law, to direct to work done by people.
] <p-residual-share>

Today about 60 percent of American national income is paid for work. How much of all spending would people direct to a person when a machine could do the task as well and for less? // CHECK labor share figure

The evidence of the past fifty years is not encouraging. People said they valued the bank teller and then used the cash machine. They valued the travel agent, the shop assistant and the local newspaper, and then used the website. Wherever a machine has offered roughly the same result at a lower price, most buyers have taken it most of the time. The preference for a human is real, and it is strongest in a fairly narrow range of things, mostly in the six categories above. A generous guess is that it covers a tenth of spending. A less generous one is a few percent.

History offers a check. In societies where a small class held most of the income, the largest occupation was usually service to that class. In England and Wales in 1901, domestic service was the largest single category of employment, with around one and a half million indoor servants. That is what an economy does with people when the main source of demand for their work is the households of the rich. It employed them in large numbers and at low pay, because many competed for the places. // CHECK domestic service numbers

#proposition([Human-required work reflects the distribution of income and does not correct it], from: [@p-residual-share, @p-ownership])[
  The pay for human-required work is set by how much those with income choose to spend on it, divided among everyone who seeks such work. If income is concentrated, many people compete to serve few, and pay is low for all but the most sought-after. If income is widely spread, people buy human services from one another, and pay can be respectable. In both cases the distribution of non-wage income comes first, and the wages follow from it.
] <p-residual>

These are two very different societies, and it is worth picturing each.

In the first, the owners of the machine economy are the only people with much to spend. Around them grows an economy of attendants: household staff, private tutors, security, entertainers, craftspeople, companions. A few performers and personalities with a mass audience become very rich, since one star can serve millions. Everyone else competes for the remaining places.

In the second, every household has an income from some other source, and people spend part of it on each other. One person coaches the neighborhood children at football and pays another to teach her the cello. There are more musicians, more small restaurants where the owner cooks, more teachers with five pupils. None of this is necessary in the sense that machines could not do it. People do it because they value doing it and value having it done by someone they know. This is a real economy, with prices and earnings, sitting on top of the machine economy, and it could be a good one. But notice that it exists only _because_ incomes were already spread. It is a result of the answer to the distribution question and cannot be the answer.

== A twist: scarce things attract spending

There is one argument for a larger role for human work in the long run, and it deserves a hearing, because it comes from the same principle as everything else in this guide.

As machine-made things become very cheap, people do not necessarily spend less on them in total, but past experience says the share of spending going to cheap, plentiful things tends to fall and the share going to scarce things tends to rise. This is Baumol's pattern again. In a society ten times richer than ours, where every manufactured thing costs almost nothing, a large part of spending might go to the two things that remain scarce: land and people.

The arithmetic is worth doing. Suppose the American economy grew to ten times its present size, and just 5 percent of spending went to human-required work. That 5 percent would be about half of today's entire national wage bill. Divided among a workforce the size of today's, it would pay roughly half of today's average wage, in a world where most things cost far less. // CHECK arithmetic: GDP ~30T, wage bill ~ 12-13T

So in the far future of a very rich post-labor economy, human-required work could amount to something substantial, especially if incomes are broadly spread. Two cautions apply. It depends on the economy first growing many times over, which lies beyond the ten-year horizon of Part IV. And it is still governed by @p-residual: the spending has to come from people who already have incomes.

== Owning and wanting

One last candidate for "what people still do" is often put forward: people will be the entrepreneurs and the directors, deciding what the machines should make.

Under the full premise this does not survive as work. Running a company, spotting an opportunity and allocating capital are tasks, and the premise is that machines can do tasks. What remains, after the tasks are subtracted, is a position. An owner is the one on whose behalf the machines act and to whom the proceeds are paid. A customer is the one whose wants production serves. A citizen is the one whose voice counts in setting the rules. These are roles a person holds because of what they own, what income they have and what rights they hold. They are the three ways in which people differ from horses, listed at the end of @ch-history, and they are positions of entitlement. They cannot be earned by effort after the fact. Whether most people hold them, and how securely, is the question the rest of this guide turns on.

== A note on purpose

This guide is about economics, and I will not pretend to settle what people would do with lives that did not need to be spent working. But one piece of evidence bears on the forecasts that follow.

People who lose their jobs become much less happy, by more than the loss of income explains, and they do not fully adapt. People who retire on a secure pension do not show this effect, and neither, as far as anyone can tell, did the leisured classes of earlier centuries. The difference seems to lie in whether the absence of work is chosen, respected and financially secure, or imposed, stigmatized and precarious. That suggests the damage is done less by the absence of work than by the terms on which it is absent. It also suggests, as a matter of political prediction, that societies will fight hard to keep "jobs" in existence long after the work is needed, because a job is how people currently receive not only income but standing. This will matter in Part IV. // CHECK wellbeing evidence

#checkpoint[
  - Under the premise, wages are paid only where a person is wanted as a person or required by law (@d-human-required).
  - The wage share of income then equals the share of spending people choose to direct to such work (@p-residual-share), which on present evidence is a small fraction of today's wage share.
  - This work mirrors the distribution of other income (@p-residual). With concentrated income it is an economy of servants and stars. With broad income it is an economy of people doing things for one another.
  - So Part II ends with a problem and no solution inside the market. Production is abundant; the channel that connected most people to it is closed; what is left of paid work depends on a distribution of income that has to come from somewhere else. Where it comes from is a question of power.
]
